#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179416 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "ccfb8833-f270-5855-8c49-fb3b6b6662d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18776" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation $5^{x+2}=9^{x-2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm\n\n$$\n\\left(\\ln\\right)\n$$\n\nof both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{x + 2} &= 9^{x - 2} \\\\\n\\ln (5^{x + 2}) &= \\ln (9^{x - 2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(x+2)\\ln(5) = (x-2)\\ln(9)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 2)\\ln (5) &= (x - 2)\\ln (9) \\\\\nx\\ln (5) + 2\\ln (5) &= x\\ln (9) - 2\\ln (9) \\\\\nx\\ln (5) - x\\ln (9) &= -2\\ln (9) - 2\\ln (5) \\\\\nx\\ln (9) - x\\ln (5) &= 2\\ln (9) + 2\\ln (5) \\\\\nx(\\ln (9) - \\ln (5)) &= 2(\\ln (5) + \\ln (9)) \\\\\nx &= (2(\\ln (5) + \\ln (9)))/(\\ln (9) - \\ln (5))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "453d4ec3-98f5-5db1-a046-a6b15c13049d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4097" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of $\\log_2$, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $2^{x+1}=3^x$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we take $\\log_2$ on both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{x + 1} &= 3^{x} \\\\\n\\log_{2} (2^{x + 1}) &= \\log_{2} (3^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\log_b \\left(b^c \\right) = c\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left(3^{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x \\log_2 \\left(3\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= x\\log_{2} (3) \\\\\nx\\log_{2} (3) - x &= 1 \\\\\nx(\\log_{2} (3) - 1) &= 1 \\\\\nx &= (1)/(\\log_{2} (3) - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/id #uuid "fbb90aa1-5adf-5c6d-8499-68eea953cc42" :question/math-academy-id "e-4098" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $e^{x+1}=2^x\\cdot 5$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm\n\n$$\n\\left(\\ln\\right)\n$$\n\nof both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x + 1} &= 2^{x} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{x + 1}) &= \\ln (2^{x} \\cdot 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\ln \\left(e^a \\right) = a\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 =\\ln \\left(2^{x}\\cdot 5\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= \\ln (2^{x}) + \\ln (5) \\\\\nx + 1 &= x\\ln (2) + \\ln (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= x\\ln (2) + \\ln (5) \\\\\nx - x\\ln (2) &= \\ln (5) - 1 \\\\\nx(1 - \\ln (2)) &= \\ln (5) - 1 \\\\\nx &= (\\ln (5) - 1)/(1 - \\ln (2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
